{
    "background_story": "Julissa was a brilliant and eccentric writer, known for her love of literary devices. She spent most of her days holed up in her small, cluttered apartment, surrounded by stacks of dusty books and scribbled notes. Her magnum opus, a sweeping novel about love and loss, was her most ambitious project yet. Julissa was particularly fond of using epanalepsis, a rhetorical device in which a sentence or phrase is repeated at the beginning and end of a passage. She wove this device throughout her narrative, creating a sense of circularity and closure. As she typed the final words of her manuscript, Julissa felt a sense of satisfaction wash over her. 'In the end, it was just the beginning,' she wrote, echoing the phrase that had opened her story. 'In the end, it was just the beginning.' With a smile, she closed her laptop, knowing that her masterpiece was complete.",
